[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 4. Today we revisit my conversation with Dave Hoover, co-founder of Obtiva, co-author of O'Reilly's Apprenticeship Patterns with Adewale Oshineye, and later co-founder of Dev Bootcamp. I met Dave at the inaugural Software Craftsmanship North America conference in 2009, and joining Obtiva that August was one of the defining turning points of my engineering career.\n",
  "Before Dave entered software engineering, he trained in psychology and family therapy. You could feel that background in everything Obtiva built. Starting in 2007, Dave and Kevin Taylor opened up Obtiva's internal study groups into Geekfest, a weekly hands-on coding evening where developers across Chicagoland practiced test-driven development and refactoring together. When I joined Obtiva in August 2009, they put me on their sole .NET consulting project first, carrying that Dell Studio XPS laptop with the monkey typing sticker that reminded us typing is not the bottleneck, while pairing me into Ruby, Rails, and Vim on engagements at Leapfrog Online, Sears, and Groupon.\n",
  "Our core inquiry today comes straight out of Dave and Adewale's book Apprenticeship Patterns: What happens to an engineering team when it becomes mathematically and psychologically safe to expose your ignorance? In corporate hierarchies, engineers are often rewarded for bluffing certainty. In a real workshop, growth only starts when you can say 'I do not know how this works yet, show me how to test it,' and then record what you learn for the next person coming up the ladder.\n",
  "Let us roll the archive interview with Dave Hoover (YouTube ID: MytNNjBsBBk). Listen to how Dave connects Geekfest, the patterns of software apprenticeship, and the human dynamics of building software teams that learn continuously on the job.\n",
  "Why do the patterns in Dave Hoover's book matter so much in 2026? Because generative AI makes it dangerously easy to fake competence without understanding the underlying system. Patterns like 'Expose Your Ignorance', 'Breakable Toys', 'Read Constantly', and 'Record What You Learn' are the exact antidotes to copy-paste AI slop. When you build small breakable toys and document your actual verification steps, you turn black-box outputs into durable engineering knowledge.\n",
  "Thank you for joining Episode 4 of The Sound Above. You can explore the full transcript with Dave Hoover and our Obtiva timeline history at just3ws.com. To help underwrite our independent compute and archive preservation, support us at patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 5, we close Movement 1 with Ryan Gerry and Jim Suchy on how we brought that workshop spirit 50 miles northwest to found Software Craftsmanship McHenry County. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]